{"name":"Damson","hex":"#854c65","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"damson","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A subspecies of plum tree, Prunus domestica subsp. insititia, native to Eurasia.","The edible fruit of this tree."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["The color of the fruit of this tree, a very deep purple."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/damson"}],"entries":[{"title":"Damson","blocks":[{"text":"The damson, damson plum, or damascene, is an edible drupaceous fruit, a subspecies of the plum tree. Varieties of insititia are found across Europe, but the name damson is derived from and most commonly applied to forms that are native to Great Britain. Damsons are small, ovoid, plum-like fruit with a distinctive, somewhat astringent taste, and are widely used for culinary purposes, particularly in fruit preserves and jams."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/Damson_plum_fruit.jpg/330px-Damson_plum_fruit.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Damson"},{"title":"Damson (horse)","blocks":[{"text":"Damson is an Irish Thoroughbred racehorse and broodmare. She showed her best form as a two-year-old in 2004 when she was unbeaten in her first four races including the Fillies' Sprint Stakes, Queen Mary Stakes and Phoenix Stakes. On her only subsequent start that year she finished third in the Cheveley Park Stakes. At the end of the year she was rated the second-best filly of her generation in Europe. She failed to reproduce her juvenile form in three starts as a three-year-old in 2005 and was retired from racing. She has had some success as a broodmare."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Damson_(horse)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Cvet_%C5%A1ljive_2.JPG/500px-Cvet_%C5%A1ljive_2.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Cvet_%C5%A1ljive_2.JPG/1280px-Cvet_%C5%A1ljive_2.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Cvet_%C5%A1ljive_2.JPG/500px-Cvet_%C5%A1ljive_2.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Damson flowers","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACvet_%C5%A1ljive_2.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/Darwin_Domestic_143.png/500px-Darwin_Domestic_143.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/Darwin_Domestic_143.png/500px-Darwin_Domestic_143.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Comparison of plum stones: Shropshire damson shown top row, second from left (no. 2). From Charles Darwin's Variation of Animals and Plants under Domestication","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADarwin_Domestic_143.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/be/Page_14_plum_-_Imperial_Gage%2C_Shropshire_Damson%2C_Lombard%2C_Maynard%2C_Yellow_Egg.jpg/500px-Page_14_plum_-_Imperial_Gage%2C_Shropshire_Damson%2C_Lombard%2C_Maynard%2C_Yellow_Egg.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/be/Page_14_plum_-_Imperial_Gage%2C_Shropshire_Damson%2C_Lombard%2C_Maynard%2C_Yellow_Egg.jpg/1280px-Page_14_plum_-_Imperial_Gage%2C_Shropshire_Damson%2C_Lombard%2C_Maynard%2C_Yellow_Egg.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/be/Page_14_plum_-_Imperial_Gage%2C_Shropshire_Damson%2C_Lombard%2C_Maynard%2C_Yellow_Egg.jpg/500px-Page_14_plum_-_Imperial_Gage%2C_Shropshire_Damson%2C_Lombard%2C_Maynard%2C_Yellow_Egg.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Shropshire Damson, shown at centre left. Other plums shown are Imperial Gage (labelled 1), Lombard (3), Maynard (4) and Yellow Egg (5).","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APage_14_plum_-_Imperial_Gage%2C_Shropshire_Damson%2C_Lombard%2C_Maynard%2C_Yellow_Egg.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/Various_Bottles_of_Slivovitz.jpg/500px-Various_Bottles_of_Slivovitz.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/Various_Bottles_of_Slivovitz.jpg/1280px-Various_Bottles_of_Slivovitz.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/Various_Bottles_of_Slivovitz.jpg/500px-Various_Bottles_of_Slivovitz.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Slivovitz is a distilled beverage made from damson plums.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AVarious_Bottles_of_Slivovitz.jpg"}],"sounds":{"damson":[{"type":"audio/wav","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/12/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-damson.wav?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/1/12/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-damson.wav/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-damson.wav.mp3"},{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/1/12/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-damson.wav/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-damson.wav.ogg"}]},"pictures":[]}}